//? Title: Deutsch–Jozsa: One Oracle Query, Visible Interference and the Promise
//? Subtitle: Trace the actual circuit, retain the original five oracles, and count classical simulation work
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A Boolean function on n input bits is promised to be constant or to return one
on exactly half its inputs. We want to identify which case holds. Deutsch–Jozsa
uses one logical quantum oracle application: equal phases reinforce the
all-zero outcome, while a balanced set of signs cancels it. A two-bit worked
example exposes actual gate-boundary amplitudes before retaining the original
five four-bit examples. Native traces distinguish the mathematical promise
from the legacy numerical threshold and demonstrate what happens outside the
promise. Measured callback, gate and amplitude-pair counts show the exponential
cost of this classical state-vector simulation. A sequential classical API
supplies actual comparison counts; a query advantage on ideal quantum hardware
does not make this C++ program a one-call classical algorithm.
)

\toc

> A hand-checkable four-input example

Let the basis order be 00, 01, 10, 11: bit zero is the least significant
qubit. Take f(x)=x mod 2, with truth table [0,1,0,1]. Starting at |00⟩, two
Hadamard gates produce amplitudes [1/2,1/2,1/2,1/2]. The phase oracle changes
them to [1/2,−1/2,1/2,−1/2]. The final two Hadamards leave amplitude one at
|01⟩ and zero at |00⟩. Thus the all-zero measurement never occurs in the ideal
circuit. The native diagram uses the actual trace, rather than a second oracle
pass. Its one logical oracle stage entails four classical callback evaluations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dj_interference.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::deutsch_jozsa_trace(2,[](std::size_t x){return x&1;});
  if(t.truth_table!=std::vector<int>{0,1,0,1} || t.promise!=dm::algorithms::DeutschJozsaPromise::balanced)return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(62,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3;box.height=1.3;box.font_size=20;
  d.node("h1",{0,0},"Two Hadamards\nuniform inputs",box)
   .node("f",{5,0},"One phase oracle\nsign = (-1)^f(x)",box)
   .node("h2",{10,0},"Two Hadamards\ninterfere",box)
   .edge("h1","f").edge("f","h2")
   .text({5,2},"Two-bit Deutsch-Jozsa: f(x) = x mod 2",24)
   .text({0,-1.5},"[ +0.5, +0.5, +0.5, +0.5 ]",17)
   .text({5,-1.5},"[ +0.5, -0.5, +0.5, -0.5 ]",17)
   .text({10,-1.5},"[ 0, 1, 0, 0 ]",18)
   .text({5,-2.6},std::format("Actual callback calls: {}; P(00) = {:.6f}; final peak at |01>",t.work.callback_calls,t.result.prob_all_zero),21)
   .save_svg("build/dj_interference.svg");
  std::print("| basis x | f(x) | before oracle | after oracle | final amplitude |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t x=0;x<t.truth_table.size();++x)
    std::print("| {:02b} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",x,t.truth_table[x],t.before_oracle[x].real(),t.after_oracle[x].real(),t.final_state[x].real());
  std::print("\nLogical oracle applications {}; actual callbacks {}; Hadamards {}; amplitude-pair updates {}.\n",t.work.oracle_applications,t.work.callback_calls,t.work.hadamard_gates,t.work.butterflies);
}
