> Why the promise turns interference into a decision

Write N=2^n and w for the number of inputs with f(x)=1. Applying the final
Hadamards to each phase-marked basis state gives

$$
a_y=\frac1N\sum_{x=0}^{N-1}(-1)^{f(x)\oplus(x\cdot y)},\qquad
a_0=\frac{N-2w}{N},\qquad P_0=\left(1-\frac{2w}{N}\right)^2.
$$
\alttext(The amplitude at y is the average sign with parity x dot y. The all-zero amplitude is N minus twice the number of ones, divided by N. Its square is the all-zero probability.)

For a constant function w is zero or N, so a_0 is +1 or −1 and P_0=1.
For a balanced function w=N/2, so a_0=P_0=0. The sign distinguishes the two
constant state vectors but is a global phase; measuring the register cannot
identify which constant was supplied. Nonzero measurement means balanced
only under the promise. The implementation computes the distribution, rather
than drawing a random shot. Its compatibility flag is the numerical test
P_0>0.5, not a physical measurement or a certificate for an arbitrary function.

A conventional reversible bit oracle acts as |x,b⟩→|x,b XOR f(x)⟩. Preparing
its auxiliary qubit in |−⟩=(|0⟩−|1⟩)/√2 makes an output flip contribute the
phase (−1)^f(x), leaving the auxiliary qubit separable. The C++ phase-oracle
simulation omits that unchanged qubit. The one-query form follows the
phase-kickback presentation of [Cleve, Ekert, Macchiavello and Mosca](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9708016).
Deutsch and Jozsa introduced the promised separation in 1992; the improved
form was published by Cleve and colleagues in 1998.

> Preserve all five original four-bit oracles

Constants zero and one, full parity, the top bit and the least significant bit
are the original examples. The last three are balanced. Their final peaks are
respectively basis indices 15, 8 and 1, but Deutsch–Jozsa only asks whether the
index is zero. The independent fast Walsh transform already in datamunge
provides an amplitude cross-check. Every numerical result below comes from
the shared circuit kernel. Small rounding residuals are printed separately
from the count-derived formula.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dj_original_oracles.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"constant0","constant1","parity","topbit","mod2"};
  const std::vector<std::function<int(std::size_t)>> oracles{
    [](std::size_t){return 0;},[](std::size_t){return 1;},
    [](std::size_t x){return std::popcount(x)&1;},
    [](std::size_t x){return (x>>3)&1;},[](std::size_t x){return x&1;}};
  std::vector<double> p0;double max_error=0;
  std::print("| original oracle | ones / 16 | P(all zero) | threshold constant | final peak index | classical calls | simulator calls |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<oracles.size();++k) {
    const auto t=dm::algorithms::deutsch_jozsa_trace(4,oracles[k]);
    const auto old=dm::algorithms::deutsch_jozsa(4,oracles[k]);
    const auto c=dm::algorithms::deutsch_jozsa_classical(4,oracles[k]);
    const auto spectrum=dm::algorithms::walsh_spectrum(t.truth_table);
    if(t.result.constant!=(k<2) || old.prob_all_zero!=t.result.prob_all_zero)return 1;
    for(std::size_t x=0;x<spectrum.size();++x)max_error=std::max(max_error,std::abs(spectrum[x]-t.final_state[x].real()));
    p0.push_back(t.result.prob_all_zero);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.6f} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",names[k],t.ones,t.result.prob_all_zero,t.result.constant,dm::algorithms::most_probable(t.final_state),c.queries,t.work.callback_calls);
  }
  std::print("\nMaximum amplitude residual against the fast Walsh transform: {:.3e}.\n",max_error);
  if(max_error>1e-12)return 1;
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.barplot(p0,names,"P0",{37,99,235}).abline_h(.5,{220,38,38},1.5)
   .title("Original n=4 controls: the promised cases separate")
   .x_label("original effective Boolean oracle").y_label("computed P(all-zero measurement)")
   .y_limits(0,1.15).size(1100,570).save_svg("build/dj_original_oracles.svg");
}
```
