\image(figures/dj_query_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Measured logical queries, simulator callbacks and amplitude-pair updates use distinct execution models; logarithmic axes show their different growth.)
\alttext(Two fitted panels plot explicitly transformed base-two logarithms of measured counts. One logical query stays at zero, simulator callback counts grow with n, and amplitude-pair counts grow to n times two to the n. Classical top-bit queries approach half the simulator callbacks.)

A randomised classical comparison changes the conclusion. With k independent
uniform queries with replacement, a balanced function produces all equal
answers with probability 2×(1/2)^k=2^(1−k). Declaring balanced once two answers
differ has no error on constant functions; ten queries give balanced-case
error 1/512, independent of n. Therefore the exponential separation concerns
exact deterministic classical query complexity, not bounded-error classical
query complexity. It also assumes a coherent oracle is already available.

> Contracts and reproducibility

The original two-field DeutschJozsaResult and its formatter remain unchanged.
The ordinary function still returns p0>0.5 and the raw computed probability;
it deliberately preserves the original effective bit f(x)&1 even for other
integer outputs. Promise claims refer to those effective bits. Negative or
unrepresentable qubit counts and empty callbacks now throw before evaluation;
capacity checks protect allocations. The trace defaults to at most 1,048,576
basis states, with a checked complete cap before allocation or callbacks. Zero
disables that optional cap but retains arithmetic and capacity guards. Memory
allocation and oracle exceptions propagate, without undoing prior callback
side effects. A physical oracle must be a fixed Boolean function: inspecting
a stateful callback once only documents the table it actually produced.

All four programs are complete and editable; figures are saved under local
build/ and copied into figures/. No external dataset suits this promise
problem, so the examples use explicit Boolean oracles. Independent tests
check every one of the 65,814 Boolean tables with n=0 through 4 by direct
character sums, including all 12,958 promised tables. Further checks cover
every affine parity through eight bits, complements, a nonlinear balanced
function, the shared Fourier circuit, original outputs, classical early and
worst-case exits, caps, empty and throwing callbacks, ownership, low-bit
compatibility and safe dimension validation. Numerical probabilities use
double precision: “exact” describes the ideal decision under the promise,
not exact floating arithmetic or a sampled hardware experiment.

> References

1. D. Deutsch and R. Jozsa (1992), “Rapid solution of problems by quantum computation”, Proceedings of the Royal Society A 439, 553–558. [Original paper DOI](https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1992.0167). Introduces the promised quantum/classical separation.
2. R. Cleve, A. Ekert, C. Macchiavello and M. Mosca (1998), “Quantum algorithms revisited”, Proceedings of the Royal Society A 454, 339–354. [Author preprint](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9708016), [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1998.0164). The one-query phase-kickback form used here.
3. IBM Quantum Learning, [The Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/modules/computer-science/deutsch-jozsa). Contemporary circuit presentation and distinction between deterministic and probabilistic classical comparisons. This report runs the native C++ simulator directly.
